{"lines":[{"w":0.079937922159830754,"h":0.015698751632582386,"t":"Reading","y":0.076332661603468854,"c":1,"x":0.80607070742319387},{"t":"adult talk or standard speech,' says Nairán Ramirez-Esparza of the University of","h":0.025632375605399549,"x":0.12198035868518843,"c":1,"y":0.11716734201541268,"w":0.73351084391276045},{"x":0.12374604747800258,"w":0.72427052815755211,"h":0.026682031438646892,"t":"Connecticut. 'We also found that it really matters whether you use baby talk in a","y":0.13599924931538454,"c":1},{"h":0.026119196560792757,"t":"one-on-one context,' she adds. 'The more parents use baby talk one-on-one, the","y":0.1548598281214113,"x":0.12193985207526548,"c":1,"w":0.73356236775716144},{"h":0.026328802262285955,"t":"more babies babble, and the more they babble, the more words they produce later","c":1,"x":0.12193627989312252,"w":0.75233500162760414,"y":0.17460563522955319},{"w":0.063909772237141921,"c":1,"h":0.019021698680909638,"t":"in life.'","y":0.19186046497309883,"x":0.12030075332378493},{"t":"Another study suggests that parents might want to pair their youngsters up so","w":0.70725850423177083,"x":0.12002596233349985,"y":0.23004315264856445,"h":0.024455577115895855,"c":1},{"x":0.11821164495864944,"w":0.71095998128255211,"h":0.023107041102874315,"c":1,"y":0.24884331022936534,"t":"they can babble more with their own kind. Researchers from McGill University"},{"w":0.72600041707356766,"h":0.024585001712757348,"t":"and Université du Québec à Montréal found that babies seem to like listening to","x":0.12008679519142769,"y":0.26656290582979492,"c":1},{"x":0.12006359735188123,"y":0.28528750546550918,"t":"each other rather than to adults - which may be why baby talk is such a universal","w":0.7429110209147135,"h":0.026137340322127334,"c":1},{"c":1,"t":"tool among parents. They played repeating vowel sounds made by a special","x":0.11817974895805226,"w":0.69779159545898439,"h":0.023813634554340379,"y":0.30555268288361359},{"h":0.025502184630207925,"t":"synthesizing device that mimicked sounds made by either an adult woman or","x":0.11818839804181294,"w":0.70347381591796876,"y":0.3237024009102738,"c":1},{"t":"another baby. This way, only the impact of the auditory cues was observed. The","y":0.34257814606485271,"w":0.72587209065755209,"c":1,"h":0.024017105544680506,"x":0.11826766363424913},{"c":1,"t":"team then measured how long each type of sound held the infants' attention. They","w":0.75053019205729177,"x":0.11628762981471434,"y":0.35852799374181388,"h":0.027265952774116697},{"w":0.7355064392089844,"y":0.37812942146729944,"t":"found that the 'infant' sounds held babies' attention nearly 40 percent longer. The","h":0.026256061413943765,"c":1,"x":0.1162983806083696},{"h":0.026624333221376695,"t":"baby noises also induced more reactions in the listening infants, like smiling or lip","y":0.39708156776923276,"c":1,"x":0.11816912408935006,"w":0.73738830566406244},{"w":0.75050598144531255,"t":"moving, which approximates sound making. The team theorizes that this attraction","h":0.02682532576384733,"c":1,"x":0.11629139299281954,"y":0.41664880929125403},{"h":0.024292267297669867,"c":1,"w":0.75610834757486989,"x":0.11443309347576691,"t":"to other infant sounds could help launch the learning process that leads to speech.","y":0.43658233197197482},{"c":1,"t":"t may be some property of the sound that is just drawing their attention,' says","x":0.11826673486818322,"h":0.023926448146527779,"y":0.45602273400707549,"w":0.70894383748372403},{"w":0.74671666463216146,"h":0.025840474341810693,"c":1,"y":0.4725543154922125,"x":0.11630316821892772,"t":"study co-author Linda Polka. 'Or maybe they are really interested in that particular"},{"w":0.69588607788085932,"t":"type of sound because they are starting to focus on their own ability to make","x":0.11445651832695887,"h":0.023826744045201131,"y":0.49217699120714165,"c":1},{"w":0.72237579345703118,"t":"sounds. We are speculating here but it might catch their attention because they","c":1,"y":0.50879427919563258,"h":0.027669353939530517,"x":0.11442878991515898},{"t":"recognize it as a sound they could possibly make.?","c":1,"x":0.11267262929185397,"y":0.531528422772791,"w":0.46257553100585935,"h":0.021121578376214978},{"t":"In a study published in Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences, a total","c":1,"h":0.028703646589231302,"y":0.56518098836058339,"w":0.74305216471354174,"x":0.1068362793501431},{"y":0.5853856623646283,"c":1,"w":0.75237782796223962,"x":0.11253967600963412,"h":0.026161034185504084,"t":"of 57 babies from two slightly different age groups - seven months and eleven and"},{"w":0.74867116292317704,"h":0.028031464016061491,"x":0.11254016388239563,"c":1,"t":"a half months - were played a number of syllables from both their native language","y":0.60201870312370653},{"t":"(English) and a non-native tongue (Spanish). The infants were placed in a brain-","y":0.6233574630966392,"w":0.73535690307617185,"h":0.024376539437907518,"x":0.1107100251607064,"c":1},{"y":0.64023087432279879,"w":0.75610946655273437,"t":"activation scanner that recorded activity in a brain region known to guide the motor","h":0.027222963125569155,"x":0.11069951289783186,"c":1},{"h":0.025423837183984888,"w":0.72230555216471359,"c":1,"x":0.11252610244896412,"y":0.66053751587336262,"t":"movements that produce speech. The results suggest that listening to baby talk"},{"x":0.11073361844595164,"h":0.023170468735065453,"t":"prompts infant brains to start practicing their language skills. 'Finding activation in","w":0.74095418294270832,"c":1,"y":0.68038818141924906},{"x":0.11062994642773835,"h":0.03042584116506486,"w":0.72806401570638013,"t":"motor areas of the brain when infants are simply listening is significant, because","y":0.69486665670317027,"c":1},{"h":0.02447224093189726,"c":1,"t":"it means the baby brain is engaged in trying to talk back right from the start, and","y":0.71729662448504949,"w":0.72791814168294278,"x":0.1106316764917447},{"x":0.11067082887072408,"w":0.75990666707356769,"h":0.027109731955445415,"c":1,"y":0.73373018007979152,"t":"suggests that seven-month-olds' brains are already trying to figure out how to make"},{"x":0.10902255507196401,"y":0.75581395364083892,"h":0.020425582807139242,"t":"the right movements that will produce words,' says co-author Patricia Kuhl. Another","w":0.75939849853515629,"c":1},{"t":"interesting finding was that while the seven-month-olds responded to all speech","x":0.10689934511694134,"y":0.77317086009731162,"w":0.72789723714192711,"h":0.024174873858056944,"c":1},{"t":"sounds regardless of language, the brains of the older infants worked harder at the","x":0.10889448921446823,"y":0.79333720200770186,"h":0.021409823828486663,"c":1,"w":0.75401138305664062},{"t":"motor activations of non-native sounds compared to native sounds. The study may","w":0.75239735921223971,"y":0.81049248945863384,"h":0.025320439974262365,"x":0.10878032407614673,"c":1},{"t":"have also uncovered a process by which babies recognize differences between","x":0.10865614042934198,"y":0.82704542055435026,"w":0.72248316446940097,"h":0.028292817449231955,"c":1},{"w":0.37283170064290361,"h":0.021650713486434991,"t":"their native language and other tongues.","c":1,"x":0.10682615729117198,"y":0.84778542774567289}],"w":2400,"h":3106}